AegisCore Sentinel — Cross-Platform Product & Implementation Plan
Document type: Product strategy + technical implementation blueprint
Platforms: Windows desktop (primary build target) + Android mobile (companion platform)
Architecture direction: Native Windows client (C#/.NET) + native Android client (Kotlin/Jetpack Compose), shared data contracts and versioned rule definitions, separate platform collectors
Product principle: Privacy-first security transparency with evidence-backed, user-controlled remediation
Status: Cross-platform proposal — validate both operating-system capability matrices on the actual target devices before feature commitments
1. Executive recommendation
AegisCore Sentinel should be a cross-platform security transparency and audit product for Windows laptops and Android devices. Windows is the first implementation target because the laptop is the primary build and daily-use environment; Android is a first-class companion client, not an afterthought or a port that assumes Windows APIs exist on mobile.

The product must use native security collectors on each operating system. Windows and Android have different security models, permission systems, process boundaries, and monitoring interfaces. A single generic scanner cannot accurately represent both. The app should share its user-facing concepts, evidence format, risk language, report format, and versioned rule definitions while keeping Windows and Android collection logic separate.
Revised product promise
Understand what is happening on your devices. See the evidence. Know what you can safely do about it.

AegisCore is not a replacement for Microsoft Defender, Windows Security, Android's built-in security controls, or a commercial endpoint protection product. It is a privacy-first security auditor and transparency layer that helps users review software, permissions/capabilities, startup behaviour, system posture, selected files, and observable network metadata. It reports only what the platform allows it to verify.
Product architecture decision
- Windows client: Native desktop app, designed for the laptop experience. It audits installed software, startup entries, services, security-provider state, firewall posture, active connections, selected files, and Windows executable metadata where supported.
- Android client: Native mobile app. It audits visible installed packages, requested/granted permissions, app metadata, supported device posture, user-selected APKs/files, and optional consent-based network metadata.
- Shared product layer: Common finding/evidence/check-result schemas, severity and confidence vocabulary, versioned rule-pack format, report format, visual language, and (later) optional encrypted device pairing.
- Separate platform layer: Windows APIs and Android APIs are queried independently. Each platform reports its own coverage, access requirements, unsupported checks, and limitations.
Recommended delivery sequence
1. Phase 0 — Cross-platform feasibility: Verify the proposed Windows and Android signals using small diagnostic prototypes on the actual laptop and Android device.
2. Phase 1 — Windows Security Auditor MVP: Build the desktop product first, without a permanently elevated service or kernel driver.
3. Phase 2 — Android Security Auditor: Build the mobile client with the Android-specific capability boundaries in this document.
4. Phase 3 — Local Device Link: Add optional, encrypted, local-network pairing so the laptop and phone can exchange selected summaries without a mandatory cloud account.
5. Phase 4 — Advanced monitoring: Add platform-specific opt-in monitoring only after independent design, battery/performance, privacy, and compatibility tests.
6. Phase 5 — Developer Security Workspace: Add explicit integrations for developer tools and AI agents; do not imply that trust annotations enforce OS-level execution controls.
Do not start by implementing a universal real-time antivirus, global filesystem scanner, or kernel-level blocking engine. First prove that each collector can return reliable evidence on real devices.
2. Product positioning and intended users
Primary users
Windows laptop users who want to understand installed software, startup programs, services, active connections, security-provider/firewall posture, and suspicious changes without replacing their existing antivirus.

Android users who want answers to questions such as:
- Which apps have access to my camera, microphone, location, contacts, SMS, or accessibility features?
- Which apps have more access than I expected?
- What changed since my last audit?
- Is this APK worth installing?
- What can I safely do about a finding?
Power users and developers on either platform who want to:
- Review the security footprint of installed developer tools.
- Inspect local APKs before installation.
- Understand package identity, signing certificates, permissions, and exported components.
- Keep a local record of accepted risks and security changes.
Product differentiation
AegisCore should not compete by claiming to detect everything. Its differentiator should be evidence quality and transparency:
- Every finding shows the actual observation that triggered it.
- Every finding distinguishes a confirmed fact from an inference.
- Every scan shows what was inspected, skipped, unavailable, or blocked by the operating system or current access level.
- Every remediation action says exactly what it will do and what the user must confirm.
- Private data remains on the device by default.
Non-goals for the standard consumer app
The standard clients will not claim to:
- Be a root-level antivirus or kernel-level intrusion detection system.
- Inspect every app's private data or memory.
- Enumerate every active socket or process on the device.
- Detect every microphone, camera, or clipboard access in real time.
- Detect zero-day malware reliably from generic behavioural patterns.
- Block another app from executing commands or using its own permissions.
- Automatically revoke another app's permissions, delete its private data, or force-stop it.
- Prove that a device is completely secure or malware-free.
These are explicit product boundaries, not temporary UI limitations to hide.
3. Android platform feasibility: what the app can actually do
The following matrix should guide product scope and acceptance criteria.
Area	Standard-app feasibility	Revised implementation
Installed-app inventory	Feasible, subject to package-visibility rules and Play policy	Use PackageManager; request broad package visibility only if it is essential to the security-audit core purpose and policy requirements are met.
Requested and granted permissions	Feasible for visible packages	Show requested separately from granted. Explain that a permission declaration does not prove that the app is currently using that capability.
App signing identity and metadata	Feasible for visible packages	Record package name, version, target SDK, installer/source metadata where available, and signing-certificate digest.
Exported components	Static inspection is possible for visible packages, subject to API/version details	Flag risky combinations, not exported status alone. Show whether an exported component is protected by a permission.
Accessibility and other powerful services	Partial, API-dependent	Inspect user-enabled services only where a supported public API exists. Otherwise provide a direct Settings link and mark the check as unavailable. Do not infer abuse from enablement alone.
Other apps' private files	Not available to an ordinary app	Do not scan /data/data, other apps' private directories, or arbitrary system paths. Android 11+ explicitly blocks access to other apps' private directories for apps targeting Android 11+.
User files and folders	Feasible with user selection	Use the Storage Access Framework. Scan only the files/directories the user selects and retain the granted URI permission only as needed.
Installed APK inspection	Feasible to a limited, testable extent	Read package metadata/signing information exposed by Android. Do not assume every APK file path is readable. Provide a user-selected APK preflight workflow for deeper manifest inspection.
