Important platform findings
- Android 11+ filters app visibility by default. Android documents QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES as a broad permission that may be appropriate for security and antivirus apps, but the app must still request the smallest necessary visibility and satisfy Google Play policy. Android package visibility · Google Play package visibility policy
- Android 11+ restricts access to other apps' private directories. Android storage privacy changes
- NetworkStatsManager reports usage history in time buckets. Access to other apps' usage requires PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS special access; these statistics are not suitable as a real-time socket monitor. NetworkStatsManager API
- Android's VpnService flow requires a system consent step before the app can establish the VPN. The operating system makes the active VPN visible to the user. Android VPN guide · VpnService API
- Android 14+ limits third-party apps' ability to kill other apps' background processes. ActivityManager API
- Foreground-service starts and types are restricted by recent Android versions. A permanent background watcher must not be assumed to run freely. Foreground-service restrictions
- The Android application sandbox isolates app processes and resources by UID. Android Application Sandbox
3.1 Windows platform feasibility — desktop client
Windows has richer user-mode visibility than an ordinary Android app in several areas, but it still does not give a normal desktop app unlimited or tamper-proof visibility. AegisCore should begin as a read-only, least-privilege desktop auditor. A small, separately launched elevated helper may be considered later for checks that genuinely require administrator rights; do not run the entire UI permanently as Administrator.
Supported Windows scope and compatibility
- Primary target: Supported Windows 11 builds, with the exact minimum build fixed after testing the user's laptop and packaging toolchain.
- Windows 10: Treat as a compatibility mode only after checking edition, build, and Extended Security Updates status. Windows 10 Home/Pro support ended on October 14, 2025; eligible and enrolled consumer devices can receive Extended Security Updates through October 12, 2027; do not assume a device is enrolled. AegisCore must clearly flag unsupported or unpatched operating systems rather than quietly marking them healthy. Microsoft: Windows 10 support has ended
- Avoid kernel drivers, boot-start components, and persistent elevated services in the first release.
Audit area	Windows user-mode feasibility	Revised implementation and limitations
Installed software	Partial but useful	Combine standard uninstall-registration inventory with MSIX/Appx package inventory. Use multiple sources and show source/coverage. Portable apps and poorly registered installers may be missed. Do not present any one registry, WMI, or package-manager source as a complete inventory. Get-AppxPackage inventories Appx/MSIX packages for a user profile; all-user queries require administrator privileges. Microsoft Learn: Get-AppxPackage
App permissions/capabilities	Not equivalent to Android	Inspect declared capabilities for packaged apps where available. Traditional Win32 desktop apps do not use the same Windows privacy permission prompts and generally do not appear in per-app privacy lists. Do not show a fake universal camera/mic/location permission matrix for all .exe apps. Microsoft: Windows desktop apps and privacy
Win32 camera/mic/location policy	Narrow, version-specific administrative feature	Windows 11 24H2+ documents policy controls for unpackaged Win32 app camera, microphone, and location access through administrative policy. Treat this as a later managed-device/policy integration, not a universal consumer telemetry API. Microsoft Learn: Manage desktop app privacy access
Startup applications	Feasible for known mechanisms	Inspect Run/RunOnce registry keys, Startup folders, and supported startup-task sources. Report source, command, target, publisher/signature, and whether the target still exists. Do not assume this covers every persistence mechanism. Microsoft Learn: Run and RunOnce keys
Services and scheduled tasks	Feasible with access-dependent detail	Inventory service names, binary paths, start mode, account, state, signer, and scheduled-task metadata where accessible. Some details require elevation. Do not label a service malicious merely because it is unfamiliar or automatic.
Active TCP connections	Point-in-time visibility is feasible	Use supported Windows networking interfaces such as Get-NetTCPConnection/IP Helper to map TCP connections to owning process IDs. A snapshot can miss short-lived connections and is not a complete packet monitor. Microsoft Learn: Get-NetTCPConnection
Continuous network events	Advanced, separate project	ETW and Windows Filtering Platform offer deeper system networking integration. WFP is a filtering platform with user- and kernel-mode components; enforcement and callout-driver work substantially raise complexity and risk. Do not ship a WFP callout or network-blocking driver in the MVP. Microsoft Learn: Windows Filtering Platform
Microsoft Defender status	Read-only status integration is feasible	Query supported Defender status fields, e.g. through the documented Get-MpComputerStatus path where available. Show active/passive/disabled/unknown state accurately. Never disable Defender, change its settings, or present AegisCore as a replacement. Microsoft Learn: Microsoft Defender Antivirus in Windows
Windows Firewall posture	Read-only inspection first	Show firewall profile status and selected rule metadata where APIs/permissions allow. Changing firewall configuration requires administrative rights; in the MVP, link to Windows Security or the firewall management UI rather than silently editing rules. Microsoft Learn: Windows Firewall tools
File integrity	Feasible for selected/accessible paths	Let users select folders or files, calculate SHA-256, record a local baseline, and compare changes. Add a small set of carefully chosen startup locations only after access and false-positive testing. Do not claim full-disk or protected-system-file coverage.
EXE/DLL preflight	Feasible for selected files	Inspect PE metadata, SHA-256, Authenticode signature status, signer certificate, version metadata, and embedded manifest where parsable. A valid signature identifies a signer and integrity chain; it does not prove that software is safe. No malware-clean verdict without a separately justified detection source.
OS/security posture	Partial and access-dependent	Read OS edition/build, patch state where reliably available, Defender state, firewall profiles, UAC/security configuration, Secure Boot/TPM/BitLocker status only when a documented interface provides a trustworthy result. Every check must report access limitations and source.
Process termination/quarantine	Technically possible in some cases, but risky	Keep destructive controls out of MVP. Prefer “Open file location,” “Open Windows Security,” “Open app settings,” and a guided user action. Any future terminate/block action needs explicit confirmation, safeguards for critical processes, and rollback/verification.


Windows-specific product rules
1. Do not present Windows permissions as if they were Android runtime permissions. Keep packaged-app capabilities, Win32 application behaviour, and OS-level privacy policy as different evidence types.
2. Do not rely on a single installed-app source. Some software is portable or intentionally unregistered. Show inventory coverage and discovery source.
3. Do not use broad admin access as the default. Start as a standard user. When a check requires elevation, explain exactly why and request UAC elevation for that bounded operation only.
4. Do not compete with Microsoft Defender. Read its status and direct users to Windows Security for remediation. AegisCore should complement existing protection, not disable or replace it.
5. Do not equate an open port or active connection with malicious activity. Show process identity, signer, path, local/remote endpoints, state, observation time, and evidence confidence.
6. Do not claim continuous monitoring from periodic snapshots. Distinguish snapshot audits from ETW/WFP-based advanced monitoring.
7. Do not silently change firewall rules, startup entries, services, registry values, or files. User approval is required, and destructive changes should remain out of MVP.
Windows-specific high-value modules
- Software Inventory: Win32 and MSIX/Appx inventory, publisher/signature, version, install/update metadata when available, and newly detected software.
- Startup & Persistence Review: startup registry entries, Startup folders, services, and scheduled tasks; baseline changes and explain why each entry starts automatically.
- Process & Connection Explorer: current process metadata and point-in-time TCP connections mapped to process IDs; show unknown/unavailable data honestly.
- Security Posture: Windows build/patch freshness, Defender mode/status, firewall profile status, and selected platform security controls.
- Executable Preflight: inspect user-selected EXE/DLL/MSI/MSIX/APK files without executing them.
- Selected-Path Integrity: user-selected folder baselines and unexpected change reports.
The Windows client should launch with Software Inventory, Startup & Persistence Review, Security Posture, Process & Connection snapshots, and History. Executable Preflight and selected-path integrity can follow after file parsing and baseline tests.
4. Revised feature set
4.1 Shared Security Dashboard — Release 1
Replace the default “SECURE — 94/100” presentation with a truthful summary:
- Assessment status: Complete / Partial / Needs attention.
- High-priority findings: Count of evidence-backed findings.
- Review coverage: Checks completed out of checks applicable to this device.
- Last audit: Date and time.
- Monitoring mode: Off / Scheduled audit / Android VPN monitor active / Windows advanced telemetry active (only if that later opt-in feature is shipped).
- New changes: Software/packages, startup entries, security settings, or supported app metadata changed since the last successful snapshot, according to the platform collectors actually enabled.
Example dashboard copy:
Assessment: Review recommended
3 items need attention · 82% of applicable checks completed
Last full audit: Today, 10:42 AM
Network monitoring: Off

When no findings are returned, use:
No high-priority findings were detected in the areas inspected.

Never use “Your device is 100% safe,” “No leaks,” or “System clean and verified” unless the specific claim is actually proven by a supported check.
4.2 Android App Risk Explorer — Release 1
For each visible installed package, collect only supported metadata:
- App label and package name.
- Version name and version code.
- Target SDK and relevant install/update metadata where available.
- Installer/source attribution where available; do not treat the installer label alone as proof of safety.
- Signing-certificate SHA-256 digest and signing lineage information where exposed.
- Requested permissions.
- Granted runtime permissions, clearly separated from requested permissions.
- Exported activities, services, receivers, and providers where accessible.
- Security-relevant manifest declarations and configuration flags where accessible.
Permission presentation must have three separate concepts:
1. Requested: The manifest declares that the app may ask for this permission.
2. Granted: Android reports that this permission is currently granted, where applicable.
3. Active use: Only report this when a supported public signal actually proves it. Otherwise say “Current use not observable by AegisCore.”
Do not equate “requested” with “granted,” or “granted” with “currently recording.”

Useful rules include:
- Sensitive permission requested and granted without an obvious user-facing reason.
- Multiple high-impact permissions combined in one app.
- Old target SDK relative to a maintained, versioned policy threshold.
- Exported component that is not protected where protection appears warranted.
- A newly installed app from an unexpected source, described as a review prompt rather than a malware verdict.
- Material changes in requested/granted permissions since the previous snapshot.
- Signing identity changes, interpreted with signing lineage and legitimate certificate rotation in mind.
An exported component is not automatically a vulnerability. The rule must consider its protection, intent filters, component type, and available evidence.
4.2.1 Windows Software Explorer — Release 1
For each discovered Windows application/package, preserve the discovery source and collect only fields actually available:
- Display name, publisher, product/file version, package family/name for packaged apps, and installation source where available.
- Executable path as supporting context, not a stable trust identity by itself.
- Authenticode signer and signature status for supported binaries; distinguish unsigned, invalid, untrusted, expired, and unable-to-verify states.
- Package capabilities from MSIX/Appx manifests where available.
- Startup/service/task relationships discovered by separate collectors.
- Last-seen and change history across audits.
Do not invent Android-style runtime permission grants for ordinary Win32 applications. A Windows desktop executable may access data through application-specific mechanisms and user/session context that AegisCore cannot reduce to a universal “permission granted” list. Microsoft explicitly distinguishes traditional desktop apps from packaged Store apps in its Windows privacy documentation.

Useful Windows review rules:
- New software discovered since the previous baseline.
- Executable or signer identity changed after an update.
- Startup entry points to a missing file or an unexpected writable location.
- A startup/service/task binary is unsigned or has a verification problem (review signal, not automatic malware verdict).
- Packaged-app capability declarations exceed an understandable baseline, where the manifest exposes them.
- A critical application is unexpectedly absent from inventory sources; show this as an inventory discrepancy, not proof of tampering.
4.3 Android Device Security Posture — Release 1
Implement a compact set of checks with reliable evidence:
- Android release/API level.
- User-visible Android security patch level (Build.VERSION.SECURITY_PATCH).
- Secure lock-screen state where a supported API provides it.
- Device encryption/security settings only where a public API provides a reliable value.
- Developer/ADB state only if the value can be read reliably on the relevant platform version; otherwise report “Not observable.”
- Root indicators only as signals, never as a definitive root verdict.
- Bootloader and SELinux posture only when verified through a supported public interface. Do not shell out to hidden APIs or parse undocumented system files as a core feature.
Use separate statuses:
- PASS — a supported check completed and met its rule.
- REVIEW — a supported check returned a concerning result.
- NOT_OBSERVABLE — Android does not expose a reliable signal to this app.
- NEEDS_USER_ACCESS — a user-granted special access is required.
- ERROR — the check failed unexpectedly.
A missing data point must never become a green check.
4.3.1 Windows Security Posture — Release 1
Start with read-only checks, each with source, timestamp, access requirement, and PASS / REVIEW / NOT_OBSERVABLE / NEEDS_ELEVATION / ERROR status:
- Windows edition, version, build, architecture, and servicing/support state.
- Microsoft Defender Antivirus mode and selected protection-state fields from a supported interface.
- Windows Firewall profile status (Domain / Private / Public where available).
- OS update/patch freshness only where a reliable local source can establish the installed build and update state.
- Secure Boot, TPM readiness, BitLocker protection, virtualization-based security, and memory-integrity posture only after Phase 0 proves a documented, reliable interface on the supported Windows builds.
- UAC and other relevant system configuration only where the value can be interpreted accurately and is not policy-overridden.
Do not call the machine “secure” because these checks pass. A missing or inaccessible check remains visible in coverage. Never change Defender, firewall, BitLocker, Secure Boot, TPM, or UAC settings from the MVP.
4.4 Cross-platform APK Preflight — Release 1.1 (high-value feature)
APK inspection should be useful both when the user downloads an APK on the Windows laptop and when they select an APK on the Android device.
1. Windows: user selects an APK through the desktop file picker. Android: user selects an APK through the system file picker.
2. AegisCore parses package metadata and the binary Android manifest without executing the APK.
3. Show package name, app label, version, target SDK, signing-certificate identity and verification result where the parser can validate it, requested permissions, exported components, relevant manifest flags, and SHA-256 file hash.
4. Explain high-impact permissions and manifest findings in ordinary language.
5. Offer local report export. On Android, offer the OS-supported install flow only as a separate explicit user action; never silently install.
Use a maintained APK parser and signing-verification implementation with a security/update policy. Do not implement APK v1/v2/v3 signature verification from scratch as an incidental feature. Bound ZIP entry count, expanded size, compression ratio, file size, CPU time, and memory; do not blindly extract untrusted entries.

Label results as manifest/configuration and signature review, not a malware verdict. A valid signature verifies a signer identity and covered file integrity under the applicable signature scheme; it does not prove an APK is harmless. Without a reputable detection source or a properly designed analysis environment, AegisCore cannot claim an APK is clean.
4.5 User-Selected File Inspector — Release 1.1
Use the platform's native file-selection experience:
- Windows: standard file/folder picker, bounded read access, and explicit handling of access-denied paths and reparse points.
- Android: Storage Access Framework with scoped URI access and revocation handling.
Support only formats with maintained, bounded parsers: APK, selected Windows executable/package formats, scripts/text, archives, and common documents for basic metadata. Calculate hashes and inspect metadata locally. Do not execute files or scripts. Avoid storing raw file contents; persist only the minimum evidence needed. For archives, enforce size/ratio/time/path limits and never perform an uncontrolled extraction.

For deletion or moving, act only on a user-selected target and explain the irreversible effect before confirmation. Do not market this as a full-disk or full-device filesystem scanner.
4.6 Network Visibility — staged, platform-specific
Network visibility must distinguish a snapshot, historical usage, and continuous telemetry. They are not interchangeable.

A. Windows connection snapshots — Phase 1
- Collect point-in-time TCP connection metadata using supported Windows interfaces.
- Map owning PID to process name, signer, path, and start-time context where accessible; handle PID reuse and inaccessible metadata.
- Show protocol, local/remote endpoint, connection state, process identity, and observation timestamp.
- Do not infer data theft from an unfamiliar remote IP, an open port, or a high connection count alone.
- A snapshot misses short-lived connections and is not a packet capture or continuous monitor.
B. Windows advanced telemetry — Phase 4 or later
- Evaluate ETW for event-driven telemetry only after measuring event loss, overhead, privacy impact, and OS-version differences.
- Evaluate Windows Filtering Platform only with a separate threat model and security review. WFP enforcement/callout-driver work is not an MVP feature.
- No TLS interception, certificate injection, payload capture, silent firewall edits, or kernel driver in the initial release.
C. Android network usage — optional
- Use NetworkStatsManager only where available and after the user grants the required usage-stat access.
- Show per-app data consumption over time buckets returned by Android. Label this historical/bucketed, not live.
- High data usage is not proof of exfiltration.
D. Android local VPN monitor — Phase 4 or later
- Build as a separate opt-in feature using VpnService.
- Explain observed metadata before the Android system VPN consent dialog.
- Provide clear start/stop controls and a truthful persistent active notification.
- Do not route traffic to a remote server by default, decrypt HTTPS, or collect passwords/messages/payloads.
- Start with flow timing, protocol, byte counts, and DNS metadata only where reliably observable.
- Test battery impact, IPv4/IPv6, DNS, captive portals, private DNS, OEM behaviour, and conflicts with other VPNs.
If a monitor is not active, the UI must not say “Real-time Sentinel Active.”
4.7 Behavioural Signals — Release 2 or later
Replace generic “zero-day anomaly detection” with a deterministic, evidence-based signal engine.

Examples of defensible signals:
- Windows: a new startup entry, service, scheduled task, or executable identity appears compared with the previous snapshot.
- Windows: a startup target disappears, changes signer/hash, or resolves to an unexpected path; report the evidence and benign explanations.
- Windows: a process-linked connection snapshot changes, but only raise a review finding when additional context supports it.
- Android: a new app requests a materially different set of permissions from its previous snapshot.
- A package's signing identity changes in a way not explained by verified signing lineage.
- User-enabled high-impact services change, where supported APIs expose the change.
- A local VPN observes a documented network pattern that matches a versioned heuristic.
- A user imports an execution log from a supported development tool integration.
Do not claim to detect:
- Clipboard snooping by arbitrary third-party apps without a supported observable event.
- Stealth microphone/camera recording merely because the app has permission.
- Arbitrary shell commands executed inside other app sandboxes.
- Unknown zero-day malware from a handful of generic network or permission heuristics.
Every heuristic must include a rule ID, version, input evidence, confidence, and a plain-language limitation statement.
4.8 Trust Manager → Trust Profiles & Exceptions
Keep the Trust Manager concept, but redefine what it does.

It is a transparent risk-acceptance and review tool, not an operating-system allowlist.

For each trusted/exception entry, store:
- Android: exact package identifier and signing-certificate fingerprint. Windows: exact file hash and signer identity; package family identity may be used for packaged apps where suitable. A path or display name alone is insufficient.
- The specific finding/rule being accepted.
- A user-provided reason or note.
- Creation date and optional expiry date.
- A reminder to review after app updates or identity changes.
Rules:
- Never trust an app based only on display name or a mutable file path.
- Never suppress unrelated findings from the same app.
- Show that an exception does not change Android permissions, Windows process behaviour, firewall policy, or OS-level enforcement.
- Make expiry and revocation easy.
- Maintain the original finding and evidence even when the user accepts the risk.
For developer tools such as terminal environments, IDEs, shells, and AI coding assistants, show their observed package/file identity, signer, capability footprint, and user annotations. To monitor their internal commands, require an explicit integration that emits events; do not promise global process monitoring.
4.9 Security Timeline & Reports — Release 1
- Searchable local history of scans, findings, user decisions, remediation attempts, and observed app changes.
- Show whether each action was user-confirmed, automatically observed, or inferred.
- Export a sanitized JSON or text report through the Windows Save dialog or Android share/save flow.
- Explain that exported reports can contain sensitive software inventory, paths, or connection metadata and should be shared carefully.
- Provide delete-all-history and per-finding deletion controls.
5. Risk model: trustworthy before impressive
Do not launch with an unexplained 0–100 score
The original security score can create false confidence. A score such as 94/100 is meaningless unless users can understand the inputs, the coverage, the weighting, and the blind spots.

For the first release, use:
- Finding severity: Critical / High / Moderate / Low / Informational.
- Evidence confidence: Confirmed / Strong indicator / Weak indicator.
- Assessment coverage: Completed applicable checks divided by applicable checks that could be evaluated.
- Data freshness: When the observation was last collected.
A user should be able to see both risk and coverage. A device with no findings but only 20% coverage must not look safer than a device with 95% coverage.
Finding structure
Every finding should answer:
1. What did AegisCore observe?
2. Which app, file, or device setting is affected?
3. Why might this matter?
4. How strong is the evidence?
5. What could not be checked?
6. What is the safest next action?
7. How will AegisCore verify the result?
Severity guidance
- Critical: A confirmed, highly consequential exposure with direct supporting evidence. Use sparingly.
- High: Strong evidence of a materially risky configuration or exposure.
- Moderate: A meaningful risk requiring review, but with context or limited evidence.
- Low: Hygiene recommendation or low-impact concern.
- Informational: A capability or configuration worth understanding, not a vulnerability by itself.
Do not assign Safe as a finding severity. A clean check is a check result, not a vulnerability finding.
Confidence guidance
- CONFIRMED: Directly reported by a supported Windows/Android API or verified file inspection.
- STRONG_INDICATOR: Multiple reliable facts support the concern, but it is not proof of exploitation.
- WEAK_INDICATOR: A heuristic that may have benign explanations.
- INSUFFICIENT_DATA: Not enough evidence to reach a conclusion; normally this should be a check status, not an alert.
6. Shared UX principles and platform-specific navigation
The product should feel like one AegisCore family, not one identical layout forced onto two devices. Share colour, terminology, finding structure, status language, and evidence presentation. Tailor navigation and density to each platform.
Windows desktop navigation
1. Overview — laptop assessment, coverage, priority findings, last audit, Defender/firewall posture.
2. Software — installed Win32 and Appx/MSIX software, publisher/signature, versions, changes.
3. Startup & Services — startup entries, services, scheduled tasks, and change history.
4. Connections — point-in-time process-linked TCP connections and evidence details.
5. Inspect — selected EXE/DLL/MSI/MSIX/APK inspection and selected-path integrity.
6. Trust — scoped risk annotations, exceptions, expiry, and review reminders.
7. History — scans, changes, findings, actions, and exports.
Android mobile navigation
1. Overview — device assessment, coverage, priority findings, last audit.
2. Apps — visible installed-app list, permissions, components, and app detail pages.
3. Inspect — selected APK and user-selected file inspection.
4. Network — historical usage and optional VPN monitor; unavailable/disabled state shown clearly.
5. Trust — scoped risk annotations, exceptions, expiry, and review reminders.
6. History — scans, changes, findings, actions, and exports.
First-launch onboarding
1. Explain that AegisCore is a security auditor/transparency companion, not a replacement for Windows Security, Microsoft Defender, or Android's built-in protections.
2. Explain local processing and exactly what is stored.
3. Windows: launch as a standard user; explain that certain optional checks may need a bounded UAC approval.
4. Android: request only permissions/access necessary for the first audit; explain package visibility and any special access.
5. Offer advanced access only when the user opens the feature that needs it.
6. Show a short platform-specific “What AegisCore cannot inspect” page.
Do not request every possible permission, run as Administrator by default, or ask the user to disable existing security software during onboarding.
Finding card/detail layout
- Severity and evidence confidence.
- App/file/process/device target with a source identifier.
- One-line observation and timestamp.
- “Why this matters” explanation.
- Exact evidence and data source.
- Coverage or platform limitation.
- Recommended action with the expected effect.
- Action status and a later verification result.
Remediation wording
Prefer:
- Windows: “Open Windows Security,” “Review this startup entry,” “Open file location,” or “Open firewall settings.”
- Android: “Open Android app settings,” “Review permission in Settings,” or “Start Android's uninstall confirmation flow.”
- Both: “Inspect this selected file,” “Export this finding,” and “Re-scan to verify the result.”
Do not promise automatic permission revocation, process blocking, quarantine, or deletion unless a specific platform API and user-confirmed flow actually support it. No silent changes to firewall rules, services, startup entries, registry values, app permissions, or files.
Visual design
Keep the premium dark security aesthetic from the original plan, but reduce alarming neon and animation. Suggested palette:
- Background: #0B0F19
- Elevated surface: #131B2E
- Border: #263247
- Complete/healthy check: #10B981
- Review: #F59E0B
- High severity: #EF4444
- Developer/trust: #6366F1
Use colour as a status aid, not a substitute for words. Support keyboard navigation and screen readers on Windows, dynamic text size and screen readers on Android, strong contrast, and reduced motion. Use monospace typography only for package identifiers, paths, process IDs, hashes, and technical evidence.
6.1 Cross-platform product behaviour
The same product concepts must appear on Windows and Android, but the actual checks shown under them must be platform-specific.
