The app must never show a cross-platform feature as “supported” merely because it exists on the other platform. For example, Android's requested/granted runtime-permission model must not be copied onto ordinary Win32 software; Windows TCP connection snapshots must not be described as equivalent to an Android VPN monitor.
6.2 Optional paired-device experience — later phase
AegisCore can eventually provide a “My Devices” view showing both the Windows laptop and Android phone after the user explicitly pairs them.

Requirements:
- Pair locally with a one-time QR code or equivalent authenticated device-key exchange.
- Use end-to-end authenticated encryption for device-to-device transfer.
- No mandatory account, public cloud, analytics endpoint, or remote command channel.
- Sync only user-selected summaries by default; never sync raw file contents, network payloads, full process command lines, or complete application inventories without a clear opt-in.
- Show each device's last successful report time and coverage independently.
- Let the user revoke a paired device and erase pairing keys locally.
- Do not allow remote remediation or arbitrary command execution from the other device.
Start with manual export/import of signed report files if that achieves the user value with less complexity. Add live local pairing only after both native clients are stable.
7. Cross-platform technical architecture
Recommended architecture: native collectors, shared contracts
Do not force the same UI framework or native system collector onto both operating systems. The most reliable first architecture is:
- Windows desktop: C#/.NET application with a native Windows UI (WinUI 3 for a Windows 11-focused product; WPF is a practical alternative if broader legacy compatibility is required). Use documented Windows APIs and narrowly scoped adapters.
- Android mobile: Kotlin + Jetpack Compose, Android public APIs, Room, WorkManager only for deferrable work, and optional VpnService in a later release.
- Shared product contracts: Versioned JSON schemas for ScanRun, CheckResult, EvidenceRecord, Finding, TrustException, report export, and rule-pack definitions. Use shared test fixtures to keep rule semantics consistent.
- Separate platform collectors: Native Windows and Android collectors return evidence in the common schema. Each collector owns its OS/API checks and limitation messages.
- Local storage: SQLite on each device. Android uses Room; Windows uses a maintained SQLite provider such as Microsoft.Data.Sqlite. Keep separate local databases; do not build cloud sync into the first release.
This design shares the product logic that should be shared without pretending the operating systems expose the same capabilities. Do not add Kotlin Multiplatform UI, Rust FFI, a backend, or a Windows kernel driver just to achieve “one codebase.” Revisit a shared native core only when duplicate tested logic becomes a real maintenance problem.
